{
  "dom_id": "step-q341256",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14198219/assets/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14198219/assets/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$",
